+# Agent Note: OAuth authorization for Streamable HTTP MCP servers
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-30-mcp-oauth-authorization.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Remote MCP servers that sit behind an OAuth 2.1 identity provider — Cloudflare's
+`mcp.cloudflare.com/*`, GitHub's, and every other MCP authorization-spec
+deployment — answer an unauthenticated Streamable HTTP connect with `401` and a
+`WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata="…"` challenge. `dsh-mcp-client`
+constructed `StreamableHTTPClientTransport` with headers only, so such servers
+were permanently unusable: the mount failed with a generic "initial connection or
+tool synchronization failed", the log carried no hint that authorization was the
+cause, and the only workaround was obtaining a bearer token out-of-band (many of
+these servers do not offer static tokens at all — they require the full
+discovery → dynamic registration → PKCE browser flow). Servers without a
+challenge, like Cloudflare's docs server, worked fine, which made the failures
+look arbitrary.
+
+## Decision
+
+`streamable-http` servers accept an opt-in `auth` config block
+(`auth.enabled`, `auth.scope`, `auth.storage.callbackPort`). With it enabled,
+the transport carries an MCP SDK `OAuthClientProvider`; the SDK performs the
+whole protocol on the first `401` — RFC 9728 protected-resource discovery,
+authorization-server metadata lookup, RFC 7591 dynamic client registration,
+PKCE, token exchange, and refresh. Two application-owned parts live in the new
+`oauth.ts`:
+
+- **State**: client information and tokens persist as a `mcp-auth/<serverName>`
+  grant record through the harness credentials seam. When `ctx.credentials` is
+  mounted, that lands in the managed `.credentials.yaml` (`0600`, file-locked,
+  refresh-safe across processes, visible to the surfaces that already manage
+  credentials); the provider re-reads on every access, so an external edit or a
+  second process's refresh is picked up immediately, and a foreign overwrite of
+  the record reads as absent rather than stale. Without the service, tokens live
+  in process memory only. The PKCE verifier never persists — it is consumed
+  within one browser leg.
+- **The human leg**: `redirectToAuthorization` binds a one-use loopback listener
+  on `127.0.0.1` (ephemeral port by default; RFC 8252 §7.3 permits per-request
+  ports), opens the system browser, and answers the landing request. The code
+  flows to the *same transport generation* through its `finishAuth`, per the
+  SDK's session model. A leg not completed within five minutes, a callback
+  carrying an OAuth `error`, or an unopenable browser (logged, with the URL, so
+  a manual paste is possible) fails the attempt; the supervisor's existing
+  reconnect loop retries it, reopening the browser.
+
+`createTransport` resolves the credentials store through the plugin `apply`
+(`ctx.get('credentials')` — optional per the packages convention) and hands it
+to each generation's provider; transport lifetime rules are unchanged.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+- **Static `Authorization` header via overrides** — the market plugin already
+  supports `headers` overrides, and servers that mint personal-access tokens
+  work that way today. It loses for the growing set of MCP servers that only
+  speak the authorization spec (no static token exists), and it teaches users
+  to paste secrets into config instead of the browser.
+- **A separate `mcp-oauth` plugin the market mounts per server** — rejected:
+  authorization is a transport concern of one connection, the SDK's provider
+  interface hangs off the transport options, and a second plugin would need its
+  own lifecycle, config surface, and a way to share the code exchange with a
+  transport it does not own.
+- **Device-code flow instead of loopback redirect** — some authorization
+  servers support it, but the MCP authorization spec standardizes the
+  authorization-code + PKCE shape via the challenge's `resource_metadata`, and
+  the SDK's `auth()` implements exactly that. A device flow would be a second
+  protocol path with its own polling and cross-device copy burden.
+- **Storing state in a plugin-private JSON file** — the first draft did this;
+  it duplicates what the credentials seam already provides (atomic writes, mode
+  `0600`, cross-process locking, UI visibility) and creates a second secret
+  location for users to audit. The credentials seam lost nothing by being used
+  and is optional anyway.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- OAuth-protected MCP servers now mount in the harness, opening the ecosystem
+  that previously failed with an opaque error; `mcp-auth/*` records appear in
+  the credentials surfaces alongside API keys.
+- Authorization is opt-in per server (`auth.enabled`), so behavior of existing
+  configs is unchanged, and a server that never challenges never touches the
+  browser.
+- The browser leg requires a desktop session; headless hosts cannot complete an
+  interactive authorization, and each timed-out leg costs one reconnect cycle.
+  The loopback listener answers only its own landing request and closes
+  immediately after.
+- One redirect leg at a time per provider instance: a second
+  `redirectToAuthorization` before the first resolves races on the single
+  listener; in practice the supervisor serializes connection attempts, and the
+  generation guard makes a stale leg's late code harmless to the current one.
+
+## Testing
+
+- `tests/oauth.spec.ts` pins the provider half: grant-record persistence and
+  read-through (including external edits and foreign overwrites), PKCE verifier
+  process-locality, `invalidateCredentials` scoping, redirect/client metadata,
+  and the callback leg (success, OAuth error, missing transport, late callback).
+- `tests/mcp-client.spec.ts` pins that a failed initialize with a pending browser leg holds the generation until the leg settles (success re-syncs in place; failure re-enters the reconnect loop).
+- The browser leg never reaches the OS in tests: the provider takes an optional opener injection and the suite passes a no-op, so a full test run cannot spawn `open`/`xdg-open`.
+- `tests/auth-config.spec.ts` pins the config surface: schema acceptance and
+  rejection, transport wiring with and without `auth`, and credentials-store
+  pass-through.
+- The SDK's `auth()` protocol itself is SDK-owned and covered upstream.
